# 2. Secured and monitored web infrastructure

A three-server web infrastructure hosting **www.foobar.com**. It is secured
with firewalls, serves encrypted traffic (HTTPS), and is monitored.

## Diagram

## Components

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| Domain name | `foobar.com`, with a `www` A record pointing to `8.8.8.8` (the load balancer) |
| Load balancer | HAproxy, with the SSL certificate for `www.foobar.com` |
| Server 1 | Nginx, application server, application files, MySQL (**Primary**) |
| Server 2 | Nginx, application server, application files, MySQL (**Replica**) |
| 3 firewalls | One in front of the load balancer, one on Server 1, one on Server 2 |
| 1 SSL certificate | For `www.foobar.com`, installed on the load balancer |
| 3 monitoring clients | One on the load balancer, one on each server, sending data to the monitoring service (Sumo Logic) |

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

1. The user types `www.foobar.com`. The DNS answers with `8.8.8.8`.
2. The browser opens an HTTPS connection to the load balancer. The traffic goes
   through **Firewall 1**, which only lets in the allowed ports (443, and 80 to
   redirect to HTTPS).
3. HAproxy uses the SSL certificate to decrypt the request and chooses a server
   (Round Robin).
4. The request goes through the firewall of that server (**Firewall 2** or
   **Firewall 3**), then to Nginx, the application server and the database.
5. The response goes back to HAproxy, which encrypts it and sends it to the
   user.
6. In the background, each monitoring client sends logs and metrics to the
   monitoring service.

## Specifics

### Why each additional element is added

- **3 firewalls:** to protect each machine by filtering the network traffic
  (one for the load balancer, one for each server). Each machine only accepts
  the traffic it needs, so an attacker cannot reach the other services.
- **1 SSL certificate:** to serve `www.foobar.com` over HTTPS, so that the
  traffic between the users and the website is encrypted and the identity of
  the website is verified.
- **3 monitoring clients:** to collect data (logs and metrics) from each
  machine and send it to the monitoring service, so we can see how the
  infrastructure behaves and be alerted when something goes wrong.

### What are firewalls for
A firewall is a security system that monitors and filters the incoming and
outgoing network traffic according to a set of rules. It allows only the
traffic that is needed (for example port 443 on the load balancer, and the
traffic coming from the load balancer on the servers) and blocks everything
else. It protects the servers from unauthorized access and attacks, and
reduces the exposed surface (for example it hides the database port from the
Internet).

### Why the traffic is served over HTTPS
HTTPS is HTTP encrypted with TLS (using the SSL certificate). It:

- **encrypts** the data between the user and the website, so nobody on the
  way can read it (passwords, personal data);
- guarantees the **integrity** of the data (it cannot be modified during the
  transfer);
- **authenticates** the website (the certificate proves the server really is
  `www.foobar.com`), which protects against man-in-the-middle attacks;
- is required by modern browsers, which warn users about HTTP websites.

### What monitoring is used for
Monitoring is used to observe the health and the performance of the
infrastructure. It allows us to detect problems quickly (a server down, high
CPU or memory usage, errors, slow responses), to get alerts before users are
impacted, to troubleshoot with logs, to understand the trends and plan the
capacity (when to add servers), and to check the security and the availability
of the website.

### How the monitoring tool is collecting data
A **monitoring client** (a data collector, or agent, such as the Sumo Logic
collector) is installed on each server. It reads the data directly on the
machine, such as the system metrics (CPU, memory, disk, network) and the log
files (Nginx access and error logs, application logs, MySQL logs), and sends it
regularly over the network to the monitoring service. The service stores the
data, lets us search and visualize it in dashboards, and triggers alerts when a
defined threshold is crossed.

### How to monitor the web server QPS
QPS means Queries Per Second: the number of requests the web server receives
each second. To monitor it:

1. Configure the monitoring client on each web server to collect the Nginx
   **access log** (one line per request), or to collect the metrics of the
   Nginx `stub_status` module (total number of requests).
2. In the monitoring service, create a query that counts the requests per
   second (for example, the number of log lines per minute divided by 60, or
   the difference of the requests counter between two measures).
3. Show it in a dashboard, for each web server and as the total of the two
   servers.
4. Create an alert if the QPS goes above (or drops below) a defined threshold,
   so we are notified of a traffic peak, an attack, or a problem.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### Why terminating SSL at the load balancer level is an issue
The traffic is decrypted at the load balancer, so between the load balancer
and the web servers it travels as plain HTTP. Anybody who gets access to the
internal network can read or modify it. The load balancer also becomes more
critical: it holds the private key of the certificate (if it is compromised,
the attacker can impersonate the website), it has the extra work of decrypting
all the traffic, and it is still a single point of failure. A better design
re-encrypts the traffic to the servers (end-to-end HTTPS) or terminates SSL on
the web servers.

### Why having only one MySQL server capable of accepting writes is an issue
The Primary is a SPOF for the write operations. If it fails, the application
can no longer save any data (new users, orders, comments...) until the Replica
is promoted to Primary, which is not automatic here and means downtime or a
read-only website. It is also a bottleneck: all the writes go to one server, so
adding more servers does not increase the write capacity.

### Why having servers with all the same components might be a problem
Each server runs the web server, the application server and the database
together, so they compete for the same CPU, memory and disk: for example a
heavy database query can slow down the web server. We also cannot scale one
component alone (if only the database needs more power, we have to duplicate
everything), they are harder to tune and secure individually, and a problem on
one component (a crash, an overload, a security breach) can affect all the
others on the same machine. It is better to separate the components, with
different servers for the web servers, the application servers and the
databases.
